#
# Showcase: the cohomology of SL_2(Z) with coefficients Sym^n, the boundary, and the exact sequence
# 0 -> H^1_! -> H^1 -> H^1_bd -> 0, which splits mod 13 and does not split mod 691 (Ramanujan's congruence).
# Open it in the notebook (Examples...): text cells are typeset, and each "Reading the output" panel pairs the mathematics
# with what HACS computed. It runs as a script too; every code cell asserts what the text above it states.

# %% [md] The cohomology of SL_2(Z) with coefficients Sym^n
# For $\Gamma=\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{Z})$ and $V_n=\mathrm{Sym}^n R^2$ (with $R=\mathbb{F}_\ell$, $\mathbb{Z}/\ell^k$ or $\mathbb{Z}$), HACS computes
# $H^q(\Gamma, V_n)$ from a finite triangulation of $\Gamma\backslash\mathfrak{H}$ together with its isotropy groups $\mathbb{Z}/4$ and $\mathbb{Z}/6$.
#
# > **Eichler–Shimura.** For even $n>0$ and $\ell>n+1$,
# > $$\dim_{\mathbb{F}_\ell} H^1(\Gamma, \mathrm{Sym}^n\mathbb{F}_\ell^2) = 2\dim S_{n+2} + 1,\qquad H^0 = H^2 = 0 .$$
# > For $n=10$, $S_{12}=\mathbb{C}\Delta$, so $\dim H^1 = 3$.

# %% H^q over F_691 and over Z
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
H = cohomology(symmetric_power(sl2z_model(), 10, 691));
display(H);
assert(H.dims == [0, 3, 0], "Eichler-Shimura at n = 10");
set coefficient_ring = "Z";
D = descent(1, 10);
display(D.groups_inv);
assert(D.groups == ["0", "Z^3 + Z/2 + Z/2^2 + Z/3", "Z/2", "Z/2 + Z/2 + Z/2 + Z/2 + Z/2 + Z/3"], "H^q over Z, q = 0 .. 3");
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
H5 = cohomology(symmetric_power(sl2z_model(), 20, 5));
display(H5);
assert(H5.dims == [1, 4, 0], "a small prime sees more: H^0 != 0 at (20, 5)");

# %% [md] Reading the output
# | Mathematics | What HACS computed, and how |
# |---|---|
# | Over $\mathbb{F}_{691}$: dimensions $(0,3,0)$, as Eichler–Shimura predicts. |
#   `cohomology(L)` is lazy (a *handle*); `display` computes it. The page typesets the value (`Cohomology`). |
# | Over $\mathbb{Z}$: $H^0=0$, $H^1\cong\mathbb{Z}^3\oplus\mathbb{Z}/2\oplus\mathbb{Z}/4\oplus\mathbb{Z}/3$, $H^2\cong\mathbb{Z}/2$,
#   $H^3\cong(\mathbb{Z}/2)^5\oplus\mathbb{Z}/3$. The rank 3 in degree 1 is Eichler–Shimura again; torsion appears only at 2 and 3.
#   In degrees $q\ge2$ there is only torsion: the rational cohomological dimension of $\Gamma$ is 1, and the finite stabilisers
#   contribute torsion in all higher degrees. |
#   `descent(1, 10)`: Smith normal forms over $\mathbb{Z}$, assembled prime by prime; `groups_inv` lists per degree
#   `{free_rank, torsion: [[p, k, m], ...]}` $=\mathbb{Z}^{r}\oplus\bigoplus(\mathbb{Z}/p^k)^m$. [C] `sec:integral`; validation `IntegralDescentGroupsValidation`. |
# | A small prime sees more: at $(n,\ell)=(20,5)$, $H^0\neq0$ (dimensions $1,4,0$), because $20 = 5\cdot4$ is a Dickson degree:
#   mod $\ell$ the invariants of $\mathrm{SL}_2(\mathbb{F}_\ell)$ on $\mathrm{Sym}^n$ are not zero. |
#   `cohomology(symmetric_power(M, 20, 5))`; the Dickson long exact sequence (tour step 06) explains the jump. |

# %% [md] The boundary and the long exact sequence of the pair
# The Borel–Serre boundary of $\Gamma\backslash\mathfrak{H}$ is a circle. Restriction to it gives the long exact sequence of the pair,
# with $H^\bullet_c$ the cohomology with compact support (computed as the mapping cone of the restriction $r$):
#
# $$\begin{array}{ccccccccccccc} 0\to & H^0 & \xrightarrow{\,r\,} & H^0_\partial & \xrightarrow{\,\delta\,} & H^1_c & \xrightarrow{\,\iota\,} & H^1 & \xrightarrow{\,r\,} & H^1_\partial & \xrightarrow{\,\delta\,} & H^2_c & \to 0\\[2pt] \dim: & 0 && 1 && 3 && 3 && 1 && 0 & \end{array}$$
#
# The image of $\iota$ is the **interior** (cuspidal) cohomology $H^1_!$, here of dimension 2: the cusp form $\Delta$, once for each sign.
# So we get a short exact sequence of Hecke modules
#
# > $$0 \longrightarrow \underbrace{H^1_!}_{\Delta,\ \dim 2} \longrightarrow H^1 \xrightarrow{\;r\;} \underbrace{H^1_\partial}_{E_{12},\ \dim 1} \longrightarrow 0, \qquad T_p\big|_{H^1_\partial} = 1+p^{11},\quad T_p\big|_{H^1_!}\ \text{has eigenvalue}\ \tau(p).$$
# > Over $\mathbb{C}$, and mod every $\ell>11$ with $\tau(p)\not\equiv1+p^{11}$ for some $p$, it splits as a sequence of Hecke modules
# > (Eisenstein cohomology is a complement). Mod $\ell=691$ it does not.

# %% The sequence at ell = 13 and at ell = 691
set coefficient_ring = "F_ell";
for ell in [13, 691] {
  H = cohomology(symmetric_power(sl2z_model(), 10, ell));
  C = cone(H);
  B = boundary(H);
  display(C.les);
  display(hecke(H, 2).A(1), B.A(2, 1));
  assert(C.dims == [0, 3, 0] and B.dims == [1, 1] and C.H1_shriek == 2 and C.les_exact, "the LES: dims 0, 1, 3, 3, 1, 0; dim H^1_! = 2");
}
H13 = cohomology(symmetric_power(sl2z_model(), 10, 13));
assert(boundary(H13).A(2, 1) == matrix(13, [[8]]) and (1 + 2^11) % 13 == 8 and (2 - (-24)) % 13 == 0, "ell = 13: Eisenstein 8, cusp 2");
assert(charpoly(hecke(H13, 2).A(1)) == mul(poly(13, [-8, 1]), mul(poly(13, [-2, 1]), poly(13, [-2, 1]))), "ell = 13: char(T_2) = (X - 8)(X - 2)^2");
H691 = cohomology(symmetric_power(sl2z_model(), 10, 691));
N2 = sub(hecke(H691, 2).A(1), scale(identity(691, 3), 667));
assert(boundary(H691).A(2, 1) == matrix(691, [[667]]) and N2 != zero(691, 3, 3) and jordan_type(N2) == [2, 1], "ell = 691: one eigenvalue 667, T_2 - 667 != 0");

# %% [md] Reading the output
# | Mathematics | What HACS computed, and how |
# |---|---|
# | The long exact sequence of the pair, with $\dim H^1_! = \operatorname{rk}\iota = 2$ and $r$ onto $H^1_\partial$. |
#   `cone(H).les` is one row per term: `term` (`Hc` $=H_c$, `H_bulk` $=H$, `H_bd` $=H_\partial$), `q`, `dim`, `rank_in` / `rank_out`
#   (the ranks of the maps into and out of the term) and `exact`. Exactness at a term means $\texttt{rank\_in}+\texttt{rank\_out}=\dim$.
#   [C] `sec:cone`, `thm:conewelldef`; validation `HeckeConeValidation`. |
# | At $\ell=13$, $T_2$ has the two different eigenvalues $8 = 1+2^{11}$ (Eisenstein) and $2 \equiv \tau(2) = -24$ (cusp). Its
#   8-eigenline maps isomorphically onto $H^1_\partial$, so the sequence splits. |
#   `hecke(H, 2).A(1)`: $T_2$ in the stored basis of $H^1$; `boundary(H).A(2, 1)`: $T_2$ on $H^1_\partial$. The Hecke action on the cone
#   (so on $H_c$) is [C] `sec:coneaction`; validation `HeckeConeActionValidation`. |
# | At $\ell=691$, both eigenvalues are $667 \equiv 1+2^{11} \equiv \tau(2)$, and $T_2-667\neq0$: the sequence does not split.
#   This is Ramanujan's congruence (showcase `showcase_ramanujan_691` follows it over $\mathbb{Z}$). |
#   The same two calls. `jordan_type(T_2 - 667)` $=(2,1)$; the local algebra $\mathbb{F}_{691}[\varepsilon]/\varepsilon^2$ comes from
#   `ext_record(10, 691)` (tour steps 04 and 05). |

# %% [md] Other exact sequences in the tour
# **Dickson (tour step 06).** Multiplication by the Dickson invariant gives $0\to\mathrm{Sym}^n\otimes\det\to\mathrm{Sym}^{n+\ell+1}\to Q_n\to0$,
# hence a six-term sequence in cohomology, computed and certified exact by `dickson_les(n, ell)`.
#
# **Level change and inflation–restriction.** For $\Gamma_0(MN)\subset\Gamma_0(N)$ there is the cone sequence
# $\cdots\to H^q(\Gamma_0(N),V)\xrightarrow{\mathrm{res}}H^q(\Gamma_0(MN),V)\to H^{q+1}(\mathrm{Cone})\to\cdots$, and in the normal case the
# inflation–restriction sequence $0\to H^1(G,V^{\Gamma'})\to H^1(\Gamma,V)\to H^1(\Gamma',V)^{G}\to H^2(G,V^{\Gamma'})$. Both are in the
# contract ([C] `sec:nebles`) and validated (`OrbifoldMPLevelConeValidation`, `HeckeLevelInfResValidation`); no read-only verb shows
# them in the console yet.
